    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-20842.s14">
        <fig id="article-20842.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Auricle Surface Anatomy. This image shows the ear auricle's parts, parts, including the helix and its crus, the antihelix and its superior and inferior crura, the Darwin tubercle, triangular and scaphoid fossae, the tragus, conchal cymba and cavum, the intertragal incisura, antitragus, and lobule. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Auricular__Anatomy" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-20842.s15">
        <fig id="article-20842.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Diamond Auricular Block. The dashed lines represent injections posterior to the auricle, and the X indicates a conchal bowl injection to anesthetize the sensory auricular branch of the facial nerve. The diamond block anesthetizes the auriculotemporal, lesser occipital, and greater auricular nerves, providing complete anesthesia of the auricle. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Diamond__block" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
